      "validation": {
        "test_future_timestamp.py": "import pytest\nimport itsdangerous\nfrom itsdangerous import SignatureExpired, TimestampSigner\n\nassert itsdangerous.__file__.startswith(\"/workspace/src/itsdangerous/\")\n\n\nclass ClockSigner(TimestampSigner):\n    now = 10000\n\n    def get_timestamp(self):\n        return self.now\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"max_age\", [0, 10, 60])\ndef test_future_timestamp_is_rejected(max_age):\n    signer = ClockSigner(\"offline-test-key\")\n    signed = signer.sign(b\"payload\")\n    signer.now = 9999\n    with pytest.raises(SignatureExpired):\n        signer.unsign(signed, max_age=max_age)\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"age,max_age\", [(0, 0), (1, 5), (5, 5)])\ndef test_valid_age_is_accepted(age, max_age):\n    signer = ClockSigner(\"offline-test-key\")\n    signed = signer.sign(b\"payload\")\n    signer.now += age\n    assert signer.unsign(signed, max_age=max_age) == b\"payload\"\n\n\ndef test_old_timestamp_is_rejected():\n    signer = ClockSigner(\"offline-test-key\")\n    signed = signer.sign(b\"payload\")\n    signer.now += 11\n    with pytest.raises(SignatureExpired):\n        signer.unsign(signed, max_age=10)\n\n\ndef test_no_age_limit_preserves_existing_behavior():\n    signer = ClockSigner(\"offline-test-key\")\n    signed = signer.sign(b\"payload\")\n    signer.now -= 1\n    assert signer.unsign(signed) == b\"payload\"\n"
      },
      "expected_tests": 8,
      "baseline_failures": 3,
      "source_evidence_limit": 40000,
      "issue_number": 126,
      "original_issue_body": "My team too was \"hit\" by https://github.com/pallets/itsdangerous/issues/120 not having read the change log carefully enough.\r\n\r\nDigging deeper, a colleague discovered that while `(signs) 0.24 -> 1.1.0 (verifies)` path is broken / requires a work-around, the `1.1.0 -> 0.24` path still kinda works.\r\n\r\nIt works by the virtue of 1.1.0 sending much larger timestamps (numerically), which are interpreted as being far in the future by 0.24. Thus true positives (recent messages) work, and false positives appear (1.1.0 outdated messages are interpreted as valid by 0.24).\r\n\r\nThis is because TimedSerializer/TimestampSigner only validate that `message more recent than X`.\r\n\r\nI have a gut feeling that it's unsafe (although a point was made that since both sender and recipient are under control of same party, this is hard to exploit).\r\n\r\nI think that validation should be `message in interval [now, now+max_age)`, perhaps with affordance for clock drift / imperfect time synchronisation it would be `[now - \u03b5, now + max_age + \u03b5)`",
      "baseline_tree_sha": "288fceb1735cf3154d363263954e08bfc87e220a",
